"""Chapter 1 drill items — pre-colonial administration: the emirate, the Yoruba
chieftaincy and the Igbo village republic."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_1 = [
    q("The Sokoto Caliphate was founded in 1804 by", ["Usman dan Fodio", "Muhammad Bello", "Muhammad Rumfa", "Shehu al-Kanemi"], 0, "Usman dan Fodio founded the caliphate after the jihad; his son Muhammad Bello built Sokoto as its capital, Rumfa was a pre-jihad king of Kano, and al-Kanemi ruled Borno outside the caliphate."),
    q("In the Hausa–Fulani emirate, the emir was appointed by", ["the emirate council", "the caliph, and could be deposed by him", "the Alkali court", "the district heads"], 1, "The emir held his emirate from the caliph, who could appoint and depose him. The emirate council advised and could restrain, but appointment and deposition belonged to the caliph."),
    q("The emirate council in the Hausa–Fulani system was made up of", ["elected representatives of the villages", "senior officials and title holders who advised the emir", "the Alkali judges alone", "the army commanders alone"], 1, "The council brought together the senior officials and title holders of the emirate. It advised the emir on policy and acted as a check on him; it was not elected."),
    q("The Alkali courts in the emirate system applied", ["customary law only", "Sharia law", "English common law", "the law of the caliphate's traders"], 1, "The Alkali were the judges of the Sharia courts, handling civil and criminal matters under Islamic law. Customary and English law belong to other systems and later periods."),
    q("Which of the following was the basic unit of administration below the emir?", ["The district, headed by a district head", "The parliament", "The council of elders", "The age grade"], 0, "The emirate was divided into districts under district heads, and the districts into villages under village heads. The council of elders and the age grades belong to the Igbo system."),
    q("One important check on the power of an emir was", ["the right of the emirate council to advise and restrain him", "the right of the emir to legislate alone", "the absence of any court", "the election of the emir by the army"], 0, "The council of senior officials and the caliph's power of deposition together limited the emir. He was not an absolute ruler, and the paper often asks for that check."),
    q("The Yoruba political system is best described as", ["a centralised monarchy with checks on the Oba", "a stateless society", "a pure democracy", "a military dictatorship"], 0, "The Oba ruled with a council of chiefs who could restrain him — a centralised monarchy, but not an absolute one. The Igbo system is the stateless one."),
    q("The Alaafin of Oyo was checked by", ["the Oyomesi, led by the Bashorun", "the Alkali court", "the emirate council", "the British governor"], 0, "The Oyomesi were the seven senior chiefs of Oyo, and the Bashorun was their head. They could reject the Alaafin, which is the classic Yoruba check."),
    q("The head of the Oyomesi in Oyo was the", ["Alaafin", "Bashorun", "Are-Ona-Kakanfo", "Oba"], 1, "The Bashorun presided over the Oyomesi. The Alaafin was the king, the Are-Ona-Kakanfo the commander of the army, and Oba the generic title of a Yoruba ruler."),
    q("The Are-Ona-Kakanfo in the Oyo empire was", ["the head of the judiciary", "the commander of the army", "the chief of the market", "the king's eldest son"], 1, "The Are-Ona-Kakanfo commanded the Oyo army and was expected to win or die in battle. He was not a member of the Oyomesi, which is a favourite distinction in the paper."),
    q("Which Yoruba institution gave women a recognised political voice?", ["The Iyalode", "The Alkali", "The Ozo", "The Emirate Council"], 0, "The Iyalode was the leading woman of the town, with a seat among the chiefs and responsibility for women's interests. The Ozo is Igbo and the Alkali is Hausa–Fulani."),
    q("The Igbo political system is usually described as", ["acephalous and segmentary", "a centralised monarchy", "a theocracy", "a military state"], 0, "“Acephalous” means without a single head. Igbo communities governed themselves through councils of elders, age grades, title societies and assemblies rather than through a king."),
    q("The council of elders in an Igbo community derived its authority mainly from", ["age, wisdom and the ofo title", "appointment by a king", "election by the army", "the colonial governor"], 0, "The elders held the ofo, the symbol of truth and ancestral authority, and their influence rested on age and wisdom rather than on an office created from above."),
    q("The Ozo title society in Igboland was important because it", ["commanded the army", "gave its holders status and a voice in the assembly", "collected taxes for the emir", "judged cases under Sharia"], 1, "The Ozo title was bought and conferred prestige; title holders sat among those whose opinion weighed in the assembly. It was not a military or a tax office."),
    q("Which Igbo institution regulated the conduct of young men and carried out public works?", ["The age grade", "The Alkali court", "The Oyomesi", "The emirate council"], 0, "Age grades grouped people of the same generation for work, defence and discipline. The other three institutions belong to the Hausa–Fulani and Yoruba systems."),
    q("The oracle at Arochukwu was significant in pre-colonial Igboland because it", ["served as a court of appeal in disputes", "appointed the emir", "collected the emir's taxes", "commanded the Oyo army"], 0, "The Ibini Ukpabi oracle at Arochukwu was consulted in disputes and its verdicts carried authority across Igboland — one of the few institutions with an inter-community reach."),
    q("In the Igbo village republic, decisions were generally taken by", ["a majority vote in a parliament", "consensus reached in the assembly of elders and title holders", "the king's decree", "the oracle alone"], 1, "The assembly debated until consensus emerged; voting by count and royal decrees belong to other systems. The oracle could be consulted, but it did not govern."),
    q("A major difference between the Igbo and the Hausa–Fulani systems was that the Igbo system", ["had no single ruler at the head of the community", "had a written constitution", "was based on Sharia law", "was ruled by an emir"], 0, "The absence of a single ruler is the defining difference. The emirate was hierarchical and the Igbo system segmentary, which is exactly why indirect rule worked in one and failed in the other."),
    q("The pre-colonial Yoruba and Hausa–Fulani systems were similar in that both", ["were acephalous", "had a ruler advised by a council of chiefs", "were governed by age grades", "had no courts"], 1, "Both had a ruler and a council: the Alaafin with the Oyomesi, the emir with the emirate council. Both also had courts, which rules out the fourth option."),
    q("The pre-colonial economy of the emirate was financed mainly by", ["tribute, taxes and trade", "income tax on salaries", "oil revenue", "foreign aid"], 0, "The emirate collected tribute from districts, levied taxes such as the zakka and jizya, and taxed trade routes. Salary tax and oil revenue belong to the modern state."),
    q("Trans-Saharan trade was important to the Hausa–Fulani states because it", ["brought salt, horses and manufactured goods in exchange for leather and slaves", "replaced agriculture entirely", "was conducted only by Europeans", "was banned by the caliph"], 0, "The desert routes carried salt, horses and goods north and south, and the states taxed the trade. It supplemented agriculture rather than replacing it."),
    q("The sokoto caliphate's system of government is best described as", ["a federation of emirates under a caliph", "a single unitary province", "a stateless society", "a colonial protectorate"], 0, "The caliph at Sokoto was the supreme authority, and the emirs held their emirates under him with a large measure of local autonomy — a federal arrangement in substance."),
    q("Which of the following was NOT a feature of the Igbo village republic?", ["A council of elders", "Age-grade associations", "A hereditary emir with a council", "The Ozo title society"], 2, "A hereditary emir belongs to the Hausa–Fulani system. The other three are Igbo institutions, and their presence together is what the paper expects."),
    q("The office of the Oba in Yorubaland was usually filled by", ["election by the whole town", "appointment by the Oyomesi from the ruling house", "appointment by the British", "purchase of the title"], 1, "The Oyomesi selected the new Oba from the ruling house, subject to the appropriate rites. It was neither a popular election nor a British appointment before 1900."),
    q("The main judicial institution in the Yoruba towns was", ["the Oba's court, with chiefs sitting as judges", "the Alkali court", "the age grade", "the market association"], 0, "The Oba in council heard cases, with chiefs and the Iyalode involved in different matters. The Alkali court is Islamic and belongs to the north."),
    q("Pre-colonial administration in Nigeria is best studied because it", ["explains the institutions that indirect rule later used", "shows that all Nigerian societies were the same", "proves that Nigeria had a written constitution", "shows that the British created every Nigerian institution"], 0, "The colonial administration grafted itself onto existing institutions — emirs, Obas, elders — and the shape of that graft explains the successes and failures of indirect rule in Chapter 2."),
    q("Under the emirate system, the caliph's authority over an emir was exercised through", ["advice, appointment and the power of deposition", "a written constitution", "a parliament", "the Alkali courts alone"], 0, "The caliph appointed the emir, advised him and could depose him. There was no written constitution or parliament in the modern sense."),
    q("The Yoruba system of checks on the Alaafin included", ["the presentation of an empty calabash, signalling rejection", "a vote of no confidence in parliament", "impeachment by the Senate", "a referendum"], 0, "The Oyomesi could present the Alaafin with an empty calabash, the signal that he should die. It is the most dramatic check in the pre-colonial systems and a favourite examination example."),
    q("The Igbo village assembly was usually held", ["in the market square or the village square", "in the emir's palace", "in the Alkali court", "in the Oba's palace"], 0, "The village square or market place was the forum; the emir's and Oba's palaces belong to the other two systems."),
    q("Which of the following statements about pre-colonial Nigerian societies is correct?", ["All of them had a single king", "They differed in structure, from centralised emirates to stateless villages", "None of them had any courts", "All of them were governed by Sharia"], 1, "The variety is the point: emirates, Yoruba kingdoms and Igbo village republics. A question that says “all” is usually wrong in this topic."),
    q("The position of women in pre-colonial Yoruba politics was recognised through the office of", ["the Iyalode", "the Bashorun", "the Alkali", "the Ozo"], 0, "The Iyalode represented women's interests among the chiefs. The Bashorun headed the Oyomesi, the Alkali judged under Sharia, and the Ozo was an Igbo title."),
    q("The emir's judicial authority was exercised through", ["the Alkali courts and his own court of appeal", "the Oyomesi", "the age grades", "the market association"], 0, "The Alkali decided cases under Sharia, and appeals went to the emir and beyond him to the caliph. The other institutions belong to the Yoruba or Igbo systems."),
    q("A district head in the emirate system was responsible to", ["the emir", "the caliph directly", "the village assembly", "the British Resident"], 0, "The district head answered to the emir, who answered to the caliph. The British Resident is a colonial officer and belongs to Chapter 2."),
    q("The Igbo system is called “stateless” because", ["it had no centralised government with a single sovereign ruler", "it had no laws", "it had no territory", "it had no population"], 0, "“Stateless” describes the absence of a centralised state apparatus, not the absence of law or government. The Igbo had courts, rules and authority — dispersed among institutions."),
    q("The main source of slaves in the pre-colonial Nigerian states was", ["warfare and raids", "voluntary emigration", "the age-grade system", "the Ozo society"], 0, "Capture in war and raids supplied the slave trade, both trans-Saharan and Atlantic. The other options have no connection to the supply of slaves."),
    q("The council of elders in Igboland performed all of the following functions except", ["settling disputes", "regulating relations between villages", "commanding the army in a centralised hierarchy", "advising on matters of custom"], 2, "The elders did not command a centralised army; defence was organised through age grades and village bodies. The other three were ordinary functions of the council."),
    q("Which of the following best explains why the emirate system survived colonial rule more intact than the Igbo system?", ["It already had a hierarchy of rulers through whom the colonial power could govern", "It had a written constitution the British admired", "It had no courts to interfere", "It was Christian"], 0, "Indirect rule needed a chief to rule through, and the emirate supplied one at every level. Where no single ruler existed, the British had to invent one — the warrant chief — with poor results."),
    q("The pre-colonial systems of Nigeria are important to the study of federalism because", ["they show that power was never exercised without some check", "they created the thirty-six states", "they wrote the 1999 Constitution", "they abolished the emirates"], 0, "The checks — the council, the Oyomesi, the assembly — are the local ancestors of the separation of powers, and the paper sometimes asks for that continuity."),
    q("A village head in the emirate system was usually", ["chosen by the emir from the village's ruling family", "elected by the village assembly", "appointed by the caliph", "chosen by the Alkali"], 0, "The emir appointed the village head, usually from the family that custom recognised. The caliph dealt with emirs, not villages."),
    q("Which statement about the Igbo village republic is FALSE?", ["It had a single hereditary ruler with a council of chiefs", "It had age-grade associations", "It had titled societies", "It settled disputes through elders and oracles"], 0, "The Igbo system lacked a single hereditary ruler, which is what distinguishes it from the emirate and the Yoruba kingdom. The other statements are true."),
]
